# `npm install` fails outright without this, so no clean checkout — CI, Vercel,
# a new laptop — can build the project.
#
# The conflict: react-day-picker@8.10.1 peers date-fns "^2.28.0 || ^3.0.0" and
# this project is on date-fns ^4.1.0. It has never been felt locally because
# node_modules here predate the bump and because `package-lock.json` is
# gitignored, so nothing ever re-resolved the tree from scratch. Vercel does,
# and it stops at ERESOLVE.
#
# This is a stopgap, not the fix. The real fix is react-day-picker v9, which
# supports date-fns v4 and React 19 — worth doing, and cheap, because the only
# thing that imports it is `components/ui/calendar.tsx` and nothing in the app
# imports that. Deleting the component and the dependency would also settle it.
#
# Until then: accept the resolution npm calls incorrect. It genuinely is, and
# the shipped bundle is unaffected because the package is never loaded.
legacy-peer-deps=true
